// sfn/sync — reserved name. No exports.
//
// Concurrency in Sailfin is language-level, not capsule surface:
// `routine { }` (the structured-concurrency nursery), `spawn` / `await`,
// `parallel`, `channel(N)`, and `Task<T>` / `join_all` are builtins backed
// by `runtime/sfn/concurrency/`. Nothing is imported from here, and no
// capsule wrapper around those constructs is planned.
//
// This capsule once exported untyped `channel`, `parallel`, and `spawn`
// that delegated to C bridges which silently returned NULL or no-op'd.
// That surface was deleted rather than left "parsed but unenforced"; any
// caller naming those symbols still fails closed, pinned by
// `compiler/tests/e2e/sync_rejects_unimplemented_concurrency_test.sfn`.
//
// A real synchronization library (`Mutex`, `RwLock`, `Semaphore`, `Once`)
// is buildable today — an ordinary struct holding a `pthread_mutex_t` is
// not an owned type, so the ownership checker's spawn-capture move rule
// never fires on it, and such an object can already be shared across
// tasks. What is missing is a way to tear one down safely: the nursery's
// reclamation registry records bare handles with a hardcoded channel
// destructor, and `sfn_rc_sfn_release` frees at refcount zero without
// invoking its stored `drop_fn`. The capability both gaps need — an
// indirect call through a stored function pointer — already ships; what
// remains is wiring it into the nursery registry and `rc.sfn`. Design:
// SFEP-0063 (scope and verdicts) and its successor (the reclamation seam).
//
// The shell stays so the `sfn/sync` name remains reserved. Remove it, or
// give it exports, once that seam lands.
